1: # MonteCarloMultiGPU - Monte Carlo Option Pricing with Multi-GPU support
2: 
3: ## Description
4: 
5: This sample evaluates fair call price for a given set of European options using the Monte Carlo approach, taking advantage of all CUDA-capable GPUs installed in the system. This sample use double precision hardware if a GTX 200 class GPU is present.  The sample also takes advantage of CUDA 4.0 capability to supporting using a single CPU thread to control multiple GPUs
6: 
7: ## Key Concepts
8: 
9: Random Number Generator, Computational Finance, CURAND Library
10: 
11: ## Supported SM Architectures
12: 
13: [SM 5.0 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 5.2 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 5.3 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 6.0 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 6.1 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 7.0 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 7.2 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 7.5 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 8.0 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 8.6 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 8.7 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 8.9 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)  [SM 9.0 ](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus)
14: 
15: ## Supported OSes
16: 
17: Linux, Windows
18: 
19: ## Supported CPU Architecture
20: 
21: x86_64, armv7l
22: 
23: ## CUDA APIs involved
24: 
25: ### [CUDA Runtime API](http://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-runtime-api/index.html)
26: cudaStreamDestroy, cudaMalloc, cudaFree, cudaMallocHost, cudaSetDevice, cudaEventSynchronize, cudaGetDeviceProperties, cudaDeviceSynchronize, cudaEventRecord, cudaFreeHost, cudaMemset, cudaStreamSynchronize, cudaEventDestroy, cudaMemcpyAsync, cudaStreamCreate, cudaGetDeviceCount, cudaEventCreate
27: 
28: ## Dependencies needed to build/run
29: [CURAND](../../../README.md#curand)
30: 
31: ## Prerequisites
32: 
33: Download and install the [CUDA Toolkit 12.5](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-downloads) for your corresponding platform.
34: Make sure the dependencies mentioned in [Dependencies]() section above are installed.
35: 
36: ## References (for more details)
37: 
38: [whitepaper](./doc/MonteCarlo.pdf)
README.md
1: # CUDA Samples
2: 
3: Samples for CUDA Developers which demonstrates features in CUDA Toolkit. This version supports [CUDA Toolkit 12.6](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-downloads).
4: 
5: ## Release Notes
6: 
7: This section describes the release notes for the CUDA Samples on GitHub only.
8: 
9: ### Change Log
10: 
11: ### [Revision History](./CHANGELOG.md)
12: 
13: ## Getting Started
14: 
15: ### Prerequisites
16: 
17: Download and install the [CUDA Toolkit 12.8](https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-downloads) for your corresponding platform.
18: For system requirements and installation instructions of cuda toolkit, please refer to the [Linux Installation Guide](http://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-installation-guide-linux/), and the [Windows Installation Guide](http://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-installation-guide-microsoft-windows/index.html).
19: 
20: ### Getting the CUDA Samples
21: 
22: Using git clone the repository of CUDA Samples using the command below.
23: ```
24: git clone https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-samples.git
25: ```
26: 
27: Without using git the easiest way to use these samples is to download the zip file containing the current version by clicking the "Download ZIP" button on the repo page. You can then unzip the entire archive and use the samples.
28: 
29: ## Building CUDA Samples
30: 
31: ### Building CUDA Samples
32: 
33: The CUDA Samples are built using CMake. Follow the instructions below for building on Linux, Windows, and for cross-compilation to Tegra devices.
34: 
35: ### Linux
36: 
37: Ensure that CMake (version 3.20 or later) is installed. Install it using your package manager if necessary:
38: 
39: e.g.
40: ```sudo apt install cmake```
41: 
42: Navigate to the root of the cloned repository and create a build directory:
43: ```
44: mkdir build && cd build
45: ```
46: Configure the project with CMake:
47: ```
48: cmake ..
49: ```
50: Build the samples:
51: ```
52: make -j$(nproc)
53: ```
54: Run the samples from their respective directories in the build folder. You can also follow this process from and subdirectory of the samples repo, or from within any individual sample.
55: 
56: ### Windows
57: 
58: Language services for CMake are available in Visual Studio 2019 version 16.5 or later, and you can directly import the CUDA samples repository from either the root level or from any
59: subdirectory or individual sample.
60: 
61: To build from the command line, open the `x64 Native Tools Command Prompt for VS` provided with your Visual Studio installation.
62: 
63: Navigate to the root of the cloned repository and create a build directory:
64: ```
65: mkdir build && cd build
66: ```
67: Configure the project with CMake - for example:
68: ```
69: cmake .. -G "Visual Studio 16 2019" -A x64
70: ```
71: Open the generated solution file CUDA_Samples.sln in Visual Studio. Build the samples by selecting the desired configuration (e.g., Debug or Release) and pressing F7 (Build Solution).
72: 
73: Run the samples from the output directories specified in Visual Studio.
74: 
75: ### Platform-Specific Samples
76: 
77: Some CUDA samples are specific to certain platforms, and require passing flags into CMake to enable. In particular, we define the following platform-specific flags:
78: 
79: * `BUILD_TEGRA` - for Tegra-specific samples
80: 
81: To build these samples, set the variables either on the command line or through your CMake GUI. For example:
82: 
83: ```
84: cmake -DBUILD_TEGRA=True ..
85: ```
86: 
87: ### Cross-Compilation for Tegra Platforms
88: 
89: Install the NVIDIA toolchain and cross-compilation environment for Tegra devices as described in the Tegra Development Guide.
90: 
91: Ensure that CMake (version 3.20 or later) is installed.
92: 
93: Navigate to the root of the cloned repository and create a build directory:
94: ```
95: mkdir build && cd build
96: ```
97: Configure the project with CMake, specifying the Tegra toolchain file:
98: ```
99: cmake .. -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/path/to/tegra/toolchain.cmake
100: ```
101: Build the samples:
102: ```
103: make -j$(nproc)
104: ```
105: Transfer the built binaries to the Tegra device and execute them there.
106: 
107: ### Building for Automotive Linux Platforms
108: 
109: These platforms require additional information to be passed to CMake on the command line to ensure proper resolution of all necessary include and library files.
110: Instead of being in the default location, `/usr/local/cuda/include` or `/usr/local/cuda/lib64`, you must point to architecture-specific paths:
111: 
112: `/usr/local/cuda/<ARCH>/targets/aarch64-linux/lib`
113: and
114: `/usr/local/cuda-12.8/<ARCH>/include`
115: 
116: An example build might look like this:
117: 
118: ```
119: $ mkdir build
120: $ cd build
121: 
122: $ cmake -DCMAKE_CUDA_COMPILER=/usr/local/cuda/bin/nvcc -DCMAKE_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/cuda/orin/lib64/ -DCMAKE_INCLUDE_PATH=/usr/local/cuda/orin/include -DBUILD_TEGRA=True ..
123: ```
124: 
125: ### QNX
126: 
127: Note that in the current branch sample cross-compilation for QNX is not fully validated. This placeholder will be updated in the
128: near future with QNX cross-compilation instructions. In the meantime, if you want to cross-compile for QNX please check out one
129: of the previous tags prior to the CMake build system transition in 12.8.
130: 
131: ## Samples list
132: 
133: ### [0. Introduction](./Samples/0_Introduction/README.md)
134: Basic CUDA samples for beginners that illustrate key concepts with using CUDA and CUDA runtime APIs.
135: 
136: ### [1. Utilities](./Samples/1_Utilities/README.md)
137: Utility samples that demonstrate how to query device capabilities and measure GPU/CPU bandwidth.
138: 
139: ### [2. Concepts and Techniques](./Samples/2_Concepts_and_Techniques/README.md)
140: Samples that demonstrate CUDA related concepts and common problem solving techniques.
141: 
142: ### [3. CUDA Features](./Samples/3_CUDA_Features/README.md)
143: Samples that demonstrate CUDA Features (Cooperative Groups, CUDA Dynamic Parallelism, CUDA Graphs etc).
144: 
145: ### [4. CUDA Libraries](./Samples/4_CUDA_Libraries/README.md)
146: Samples that demonstrate how to use CUDA platform libraries (NPP, NVJPEG, NVGRAPH cuBLAS, cuFFT, cuSPARSE, cuSOLVER and cuRAND).
147: 
148: ### [5. Domain Specific](./Samples/5_Domain_Specific/README.md)
149: Samples that are specific to domain (Graphics, Finance, Image Processing).
150: 
151: ### [6. Performance](./Samples/6_Performance/README.md)
152: Samples that demonstrate performance optimization.
153: 
154: ### [7. libNVVM](./Samples/7_libNVVM/README.md)
155: Samples that demonstrate the use of libNVVVM and NVVM IR.
156: 
157: ## Dependencies
158: 
159: Some CUDA Samples rely on third-party applications and/or libraries, or features provided by the CUDA Toolkit and Driver, to either build or execute. These dependencies are listed below.
160: 
161: If a sample has a third-party dependency that is available on the system, but is not installed, the sample will waive itself at build time.
162: 
163: Each sample's dependencies are listed in its README's Dependencies section.
164: 
165: ### Third-Party Dependencies
166: 
167: These third-party dependencies are required by some CUDA samples. If available, these dependencies are either installed on your system automatically, or are installable via your system's package manager (Linux) or a third-party website.
168: 
169: #### FreeImage
170: 
171: FreeImage is an open source imaging library. FreeImage can usually be installed on Linux using your distribution's package manager system. FreeImage can also be downloaded from the FreeImage website.
172: 
173: To set up FreeImage on a Windows system, extract the FreeImage DLL distribution into the folder `../../../Common/FreeImage/Dist/x64` such that it contains the .h and .lib files. Copy the .dll file to the Release/ Debug/ execution folder or pass the FreeImage folder when cmake configuring with the `-DFREEIMAGE_INCLUDE_DIR` and `-DFREEIMAGE_LIBRARY` options.
174: 
175: #### Message Passing Interface
176: 
177: MPI (Message Passing Interface) is an API for communicating data between distributed processes. A MPI compiler can be installed using your Linux distribution's package manager system. It is also available on some online resources, such as [Open MPI](http://www.open-mpi.org/). On Windows, to build and run MPI-CUDA applications one can install [MS-MPI SDK](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/bb524831(v=vs.85).aspx).
178: 
179: #### Only 64-Bit
180: 
181: Some samples can only be run on a 64-bit operating system.
182: 
183: #### DirectX
184: 
185: DirectX is a collection of APIs designed to allow development of multimedia applications on Microsoft platforms. For Microsoft platforms, NVIDIA's CUDA Driver supports DirectX. Several CUDA Samples for Windows demonstrates CUDA-DirectX Interoperability, for building such samples one needs to install Microsoft Visual Studio 2012 or higher which provides Microsoft Windows SDK for Windows 8.
186: 
187: #### DirectX12
188: 
189: DirectX 12 is a collection of advanced low-level programming APIs which can reduce driver overhead, designed to allow development of multimedia applications on Microsoft platforms starting with Windows 10 OS onwards. For Microsoft platforms, NVIDIA's CUDA Driver supports DirectX. Few CUDA Samples for Windows demonstrates CUDA-DirectX12 Interoperability, for building such samples one needs to install [Windows 10 SDK or higher](https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/downloads/windows-10-sdk), with VS 2015 or VS 2017.
190: 
191: #### OpenGL
192: 
193: OpenGL is a graphics library used for 2D and 3D rendering. On systems which support OpenGL, NVIDIA's OpenGL implementation is provided with the CUDA Driver.
194: 
195: #### OpenGL ES
196: 
197: OpenGL ES is an embedded systems graphics library used for 2D and 3D rendering. On systems which support OpenGL ES, NVIDIA's OpenGL ES implementation is provided with the CUDA Driver.
198: 
199: #### Vulkan
200: 
201: Vulkan is a low-overhead, cross-platform 3D graphics and compute API. Vulkan targets high-performance realtime 3D graphics applications such as video games and interactive media across all platforms. On systems which support Vulkan, NVIDIA's Vulkan implementation is provided with the CUDA Driver. For building and running Vulkan applications one needs to install the [Vulkan SDK](https://www.lunarg.com/vulkan-sdk/).
202: 
203: #### GLFW
204: GLFW is a lightweight, open-source library designed for managing OpenGL, OpenGL ES, and Vulkan contexts. It simplifies the process of creating and managing windows, handling user input (keyboard, mouse, and joystick), and working with multiple monitors in a cross-platform manner.
205: 
206: To set up GLFW on a Windows system, Download the pre-built binaries from [GLFW website](https://www.glfw.org/download.html) and extract the zip file into the folder, pass the GLFW include header as `-DGLFW_INCLUDE_DIR` for cmake configuring and follow the Build_instructions.txt in the sample folder to set up the t.
207: 
208: #### OpenMP
209: 
210: OpenMP is an API for multiprocessing programming. OpenMP can be installed using your Linux distribution's package manager system. It usually comes preinstalled with GCC. It can also be found at the [OpenMP website](http://openmp.org/).
211: 
212: #### Screen
213: 
214: Screen is a windowing system found on the QNX operating system. Screen is usually found as part of the root filesystem.
215: 
216: #### X11
217: 
218: X11 is a windowing system commonly found on *-nix style operating systems. X11 can be installed using your Linux distribution's package manager, and comes preinstalled on Mac OS X systems.
219: 
220: #### EGL
221: 
222: EGL is an interface between Khronos rendering APIs (such as OpenGL, OpenGL ES or OpenVG) and the underlying native platform windowing system.
223: 
224: #### EGLOutput
225: 
226: EGLOutput is a set of EGL extensions which allow EGL to render directly to the display.
227: 
228: #### EGLSync
229: 
230: EGLSync is a set of EGL extensions which provides sync objects that are synchronization primitive, representing events whose completion can be tested or waited upon.
231: 
232: #### NVSCI
233: 
234: NvSci is a set of communication interface libraries out of which CUDA interops with NvSciBuf and NvSciSync. NvSciBuf allows applications to allocate and exchange buffers in memory. NvSciSync allows applications to manage synchronization objects which coordinate when sequences of operations begin and end.
235: 
236: #### NvMedia
237: 
238: NvMedia provides powerful processing of multimedia data for true hardware acceleration across NVIDIA Tegra devices. Applications leverage the NvMedia Application Programming Interface (API) to process the image and video data.
239: 
240: ### CUDA Features
241: 
242: These CUDA features are needed by some CUDA samples. They are provided by either the CUDA Toolkit or CUDA Driver. Some features may not be available on your system.
243: 
244: #### CUFFT Callback Routines
245: 
246: CUFFT Callback Routines are user-supplied kernel routines that CUFFT will call when loading or storing data. These callback routines are only available on Linux x86_64 and ppc64le systems.
247: 
248: #### CUDA Dynamic Parallellism
249: 
250: CDP (CUDA Dynamic Parallellism) allows kernels to be launched from threads running on the GPU. CDP is only available on GPUs with SM architecture of 3.5 or above.
251: 
252: #### Multi-block Cooperative Groups
253: 
254: Multi Block Cooperative Groups(MBCG) extends Cooperative Groups and the CUDA programming model to express inter-thread-block synchronization. MBCG is available on GPUs with Pascal and higher architecture.
255: 
256: #### Multi-Device Cooperative Groups
257: 
258:  Multi Device Cooperative Groups extends Cooperative Groups and the CUDA programming model enabling thread blocks executing on multiple GPUs to cooperate and synchronize as they execute. This feature is available on GPUs with Pascal and higher architecture.
259: 
260: #### CUBLAS
261: 
262: CUBLAS (CUDA Basic Linear Algebra Subroutines) is a GPU-accelerated version of the BLAS library.
263: 
264: #### CUDA Interprocess Communication
265: 
266: IPC (Interprocess Communication) allows processes to share device pointers.
267: 
268: #### CUFFT
269: 
270: CUFFT (CUDA Fast Fourier Transform) is a GPU-accelerated FFT library.
271: 
272: #### CURAND
273: 
274: CURAND (CUDA Random Number Generation) is a GPU-accelerated RNG library.
275: 
276: #### CUSPARSE
277: 
278: CUSPARSE (CUDA Sparse Matrix) provides linear algebra subroutines used for sparse matrix calculations.
279: 
280: #### CUSOLVER
281: 
282: CUSOLVER library is a high-level package based on the CUBLAS and CUSPARSE libraries. It combines three separate libraries under a single umbrella, each of which can be used independently or in concert with other toolkit libraries. The intent ofCUSOLVER is to provide useful LAPACK-like features, such as common matrix factorization and triangular solve routines for dense matrices, a sparse least-squares solver and an eigenvalue solver. In addition cuSolver provides a new refactorization library useful for solving sequences of matrices with a shared sparsity pattern.
283: 
284: #### NPP
285: 
286: NPP (NVIDIA Performance Primitives) provides GPU-accelerated image, video, and signal processing functions.
287: 
288: #### NVGRAPH
289: 
290: NVGRAPH is a GPU-accelerated graph analytics library.
291: 
292: #### NVJPEG
293: 
294: NVJPEG library provides high-performance, GPU accelerated JPEG decoding functionality for image formats commonly used in deep learning and hyperscale multimedia applications.
295: 
296: #### NVRTC
297: 
298: NVRTC (CUDA RunTime Compilation) is a runtime compilation library for CUDA C++.
299: 
300: #### Stream Priorities
301: 
302: Stream Priorities allows the creation of streams with specified priorities. Stream Priorities is only available on GPUs with SM architecture of 3.5 or above.
303: 
304: #### Unified Virtual Memory
305: 
306: UVM (Unified Virtual Memory) enables memory that can be accessed by both the CPU and GPU without explicit copying between the two. UVM is only available on Linux and Windows systems.
307: 
308: #### 16-bit Floating Point
309: 
310: FP16 is a 16-bit floating-point format. One bit is used for the sign, five bits for the exponent, and ten bits for the mantissa.
311: 
312: #### C++11 CUDA
313: 
314: NVCC support of [C++11 features](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C++11).
315: 
316: #### CMake
317: 
318: The libNVVM samples are built using [CMake](https://cmake.org/) 3.10 or later.
319: 
320: ## Contributors Guide
321: 
322: We welcome your input on issues and suggestions for samples. At this time we are not accepting contributions from the public, check back here as we evolve our contribution model.
323: 
324: We use Google C++ Style Guide for all the sources https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html
325: 
326: ## Frequently Asked Questions
327: 
328: Answers to frequently asked questions about CUDA can be found at http://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-faq and in the [CUDA Toolkit Release Notes](http://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-toolkit-release-notes/index.html).
329: 
330: ## References
331: 
332: *   [CUDA Programming Guide](http://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-programming-guide/index.html)
333: *   [Accelerated Computing Blog](https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/?tags=accelerated-computing)
334: 
335: ## Attributions
336: 
